      <div class="hero-text">
        <h1 class="hero-title">Stochastic Macro-Driven DCF</h1>
        <p class="hero-sub">
          This tool builds on a traditional discounted cash flow (DCF) framework, but instead of
          producing a single valuation from one set of assumptions, it explores a range of possible
          outcomes. Using familiar inputs such as revenue, margins, capital spending, and discount
          rates, the model simulates different paths for key macro drivers including oil prices,
          interest rates, and demand. Each path represents a possible economic environment, which is
          translated into company performance and ultimately into an implied valuation. The result is
          a distribution of values rather than a single estimate, allowing valuation to be viewed
          across different macro conditions. In this version, the macro variables are simulated
          independently and the operating relationships are intentionally simple to keep the model
          transparent.
        </p>
 
        <!-- About this model — collapsed by default -->
        <details class="about-details">
          <summary class="about-summary">
            <span class="about-chevron">▸</span>
            About this model
          </summary>
          <div class="about-body">
 
            <p>This model extends a standard discounted cash flow framework by evaluating company value across many possible macro environments rather than a single assumed path. A traditional DCF usually begins by selecting one set of assumptions for revenue growth, margins, reinvestment, and discount rates, then uses those assumptions to estimate one intrinsic value. That framework is useful, but it can also suggest more precision than the underlying environment warrants, especially for commodity-linked businesses.</p>
 
            <p>Here, the valuation logic remains familiar, but the forecast process changes. Instead of assuming one future path for oil prices, interest rates, and demand, the model simulates many possible paths for those macro variables. Each path represents a different economic environment. Some are stronger, some weaker, and many fall somewhere in between.</p>
 
            <p>Those macro paths are then mapped into company performance. In this version, oil prices, demand, and interest rates influence revenue, margins, capital spending, and free cash flow through a simplified operating structure. The output is therefore not a single forecast built from one assumed future, but a range of implied values generated across many internally consistent scenarios.</p>
 
            <p>The value of that distribution is that it reveals more than an average estimate. It shows how wide the range of outcomes may be, how much downside may exist relative to the current market price, and whether upside and downside appear balanced or skewed. A company may look attractive near the center of the distribution while still carrying meaningful downside in weaker macro environments, or it may show limited downside but only modest upside.</p>
 
            <p>Each simulation path can be thought of as one possible sequence of macro outcomes over the forecast period. A given path may involve stronger oil prices, weaker demand, lower rates, or any other combination permitted by the model assumptions. That path then produces a corresponding set of operating outcomes and, ultimately, an implied valuation. Repeating that process many times creates the full valuation distribution.</p>
 
            <p>This framework is designed to support probabilistic interpretation rather than point prediction. It is not intended to identify one correct oil price or one exact stock value. Instead, it provides a structured way to view valuation across many possible futures using familiar DCF building blocks.</p>
 
            <p>This version is intentionally simplified. The macro variables are simulated independently, the operating relationships are stylized, and many real-world frictions are not yet modeled. That makes the framework easier to inspect and interpret, but it also means the output should be read as a structured valuation system rather than a complete market model.</p>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">What a traditional DCF does</h3>
            <p>A traditional discounted cash flow model estimates value by forecasting future free cash flow and discounting those cash flows back to the present. In most cases, the analyst selects one base case for revenue growth, profitability, reinvestment, and discount rates, which leads to one implied valuation, sometimes accompanied by a bull and bear case.</p>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">What this model changes</h3>
            <p>This model keeps the same broad DCF structure, but replaces one assumed future with many simulated macro scenarios. Instead of asking what the company is worth under one forecast, it asks how value behaves across a range of possible oil, rate, and demand environments.</p>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">Why the distribution matters</h3>
            <p>The distribution is useful because it helps show where valuation is centered, how wide the range of outcomes is, how much downside may exist relative to the market price, and whether the payoff profile appears balanced or skewed. It also makes uncertainty visible in a way a single-point estimate cannot.</p>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">What can be inferred from the output</h3>
            <ul class="about-list">
              <li>Where the center of the valuation distribution sits</li>
              <li>How wide the range of outcomes is</li>
              <li>How much downside exists relative to the current market price</li>
              <li>Whether upside and downside are roughly balanced or skewed</li>
              <li>How sensitive valuation appears to macro assumptions</li>
            </ul>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">What should not be inferred</h3>
            <p>The model should not be read as a precise prediction of where the stock will trade. It is also not a complete representation of all operating risks, capital structure dynamics, or market behavior. It is a probabilistic valuation framework, not a direct forecasting machine.</p>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">Version 1 limitations</h3>
            <ul class="about-list">
              <li>Macro variables are simulated independently</li>
              <li>Operating sensitivities are simplified</li>
              <li>Terminal value still plays a large role</li>
              <li>Cost structure is not fully modeled</li>
              <li>Capital structure dynamics are simplified</li>
            </ul>
 
            <h3 class="about-h3">Input guide</h3>
 
            <p class="about-guide-sub">Basic inputs</p>
            <ul class="about-list">
              <li><strong>Current Oil Price</strong> — Starting oil price for all simulated paths.</li>
              <li><strong>Oil Volatility</strong> — How widely oil prices move from path to path.</li>
              <li><strong>Long-Run Oil Target</strong> — The level oil prices tend to drift toward over time.</li>
              <li><strong>Interest Rate</strong> — Starting risk-free rate used in discounting.</li>
              <li><strong>Demand Growth</strong> — Baseline growth assumption for the demand series.</li>
              <li><strong>Revenue</strong> — Current company revenue used as the operating base.</li>
              <li><strong>Production</strong> — Current production level used to anchor operating scale.</li>
              <li><strong>EBIT Margin</strong> — Base operating profitability before interest and taxes.</li>
              <li><strong>Capex % Rev</strong> — Share of revenue reinvested into the business.</li>
              <li><strong>D&amp;A % Rev</strong> — Non-cash depreciation and amortization as a share of revenue.</li>
              <li><strong>WC % Rev</strong> — Working capital intensity relative to revenue.</li>
              <li><strong>Shares</strong> — Shares outstanding used to convert equity value into per-share value.</li>
              <li><strong>Net Debt</strong> — Debt minus cash, subtracted from enterprise value.</li>
              <li><strong>Market Price</strong> — Current stock price used for comparison.</li>
              <li><strong>Simulations</strong> — Number of Monte Carlo paths to generate.</li>
            </ul>
 
            <p class="about-guide-sub">Advanced settings</p>
            <ul class="about-list">
              <li><strong>Commodity Reversion Speed</strong> — How quickly oil moves back toward its long-run target.</li>
              <li><strong>Rate Reversion Speed</strong> — How quickly rates move back toward their long-run level.</li>
              <li><strong>Rate Volatility</strong> — How much interest rates fluctuate.</li>
              <li><strong>Rate Long-Run Target</strong> — Long-run anchor for interest rates.</li>
              <li><strong>Demand Index Level</strong> — Starting level of the demand series.</li>
              <li><strong>Demand Volatility</strong> — How much demand fluctuates path to path.</li>
              <li><strong>Revenue ← Commodity</strong> — How strongly revenue responds to oil price changes.</li>
              <li><strong>Revenue ← Demand</strong> — How strongly revenue responds to demand changes.</li>
              <li><strong>Margin ← Commodity</strong> — How strongly margins respond to oil price changes.</li>
              <li><strong>Capex ← Commodity</strong> — How strongly capex intensity responds to oil prices.</li>
              <li><strong>Production ← Reinvestment</strong> — How strongly reinvestment supports future production.</li>
              <li><strong>Tax Rate</strong> — Effective tax rate used in free cash flow.</li>
              <li><strong>Beta</strong> — Equity sensitivity used in the cost of equity.</li>
              <li><strong>ERP</strong> — Equity risk premium used in WACC.</li>
              <li><strong>Debt Spread</strong> — Spread above the risk-free rate used in cost of debt.</li>
              <li><strong>Debt Weight</strong> — Debt share of capital in WACC.</li>
              <li><strong>Equity Weight</strong> — Equity share of capital in WACC.</li>
              <li><strong>Terminal Growth</strong> — Perpetual growth assumption used in terminal value.</li>
              <li><strong>Forecast Years</strong> — Number of explicit forecast years.</li>
              <li><strong>Random Seed</strong> — Optional fixed number for reproducible runs.</li>
            </ul>
 
          </div><!-- /about-body -->
        </details>
 
      </div><!-- /hero-text -->
    </div>
